it has been one of the hardest hit  cities in ukraine to the point they're  now burying the dead in mariupol  anywhere they can it is surrounded there  are russian troops on three sides of the  port city russian ships on the other  the regional governor says the attacks  and airstrikes are constant  sunday the russian defense ministry  demanded ukrainian forces in the city  surrender it said if they laid down  their weapons and raised white flags  russia would give safe passage for  soldiers to leave mariupol the general  who made that offer didn't say what  would happen if it was rejected and it  was the city's mayor declined the offer  posting on facebook that russia didn't  need to give them time to decide their  answer was ready in advance the  country's deputy prime minister said  they wouldn't even talk about surrender  or laying down weapons  the reports in russian media of what  might happen if russian forces do take  over quoting defense officials they say  ukrainian authorities in mariupol could  face a military tribunal for siding with  what they call bandits  ukrainian president meantime says some  civilians in mariupol were able to leave  the city sunday about 4 000 people were  able to get out through humanitarian  corridors their plans to send more buses  back today but the city is also trying  to determine the losses after a russian  bomb was dropped on an art school crane  says about 400 people were using it as a  shelter vladimir zelinski is calling the  situation in the city a terror that will  be remembered for centuries  mike armstrong global news jeshof poland
 

